 body{margin:0} 0 · Five rules wherever a person meets credits The boards themselves sit with their flows: On boarding experience, Compositions (beside the Composer), Agent setup &amp; Preferences and Print history. Search “credits” to find them all. 1. One word, one unit, credits only. It is “credits”, never coins or tokens. A balance is a number of credits and nothing else. Prices in messages appear only on the picker options, the stop card, the pricing page sentence and one scale line under the first-month offer. Dollars appear only where money changes hands. 2. Credits move only when the person sends a message. Turns the app starts are free, so the balance never changes without a message from the person. On Workshop, Standard messages cost 0 credits. 3. Credits are named by kind, with a date. Every credit pays for either tier, so bars are grouped only by when credits expire: First-month credits, Free credits and Bought credits, one bar each, and a plan’s monthly credits the same way. After the first month, accounts without a plan get 50 free credits a month, pack holders too; the first month has only the 500. An expiring lot expires one month from the day it was granted; a plan’s credits expire with its billing cycle. The breakdown says once that expiring credits are used first. 4. Say the number before the boundary, not at it. When the balance runs low the composer says so once, “About 25 credits left”, so reaching it is never news. 5. Nothing typed is lost. A message that can’t be sent stays in the composer, and nothing stops silently. 
